  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the distance traveled when $t=2$ seconds is equal to ${d}^{\u2032}(2).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{t}^{3}+7t}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(6{t}^{2}+7)(t+1)-(2{t}^{3}+7t)(1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}+7}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{\u2032}(2) & =\\frac{4(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2}+7}{((2)+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32+24+7}{9} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the distance traveled when $t=2$ seconds is $7$ meters per second.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
